  "meta": {
    "title": "Molt Rush: Chitin Bloom in a Caspian Shallows Die-off",
    "date": "2026-11-21",
    "lesson": "When brine shrimp and amphipods molt or die en masse in autumn in the shallow northern Caspian shelf, their chitinous exoskeletons sink and create a sudden pulse of a normally scarce polymer. Chitinolytic bacteria specialized for this brackish, low-salinity inland sea respond fast, since chitin is their dominant carbon source here rather than the protein/lipid mix typical of the open ocean. Because the Caspian is isolated and endemic lineages are genetically distant from marine relatives, cross-reactive antibiotics and viruses behave differently than textbook marine examples. This level asks you to specialize digestion on chitin (carbohydrate-class) while surviving opportunistic grazers and phage pressure in a confined, nutrient-pulsed shelf habitat.",
    "citation": "Based on reports of seasonal crustacean die-offs and endemic bacterial chitin degradation in the shallow northern Caspian Sea shelf.",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Northern Caspian Sea shallow shelf, autumn brine-shrimp/amphipod die-off, endemic brackish-water chitinolytic bacteria"
  },
